2. How the 5% Threshold Is Measured

   (a) The "Five Percent Threshold" is met when the Used Portion is more
       than five percent (5%) of the whole by ANY of these measures:
         (1) non-blank lines of all counted files;
         (2) non-whitespace characters (characters other than spaces,
             tabs, and line breaks) of all counted files;
         (3) non-blank lines or non-whitespace characters of the lesson,
             quiz, scenario, and other learner-facing text alone; or
         (4) non-blank lines or non-whitespace characters of any single
             counted file that has at least 500 non-blank lines.

   (b) Every measure is taken on the Original Work's own files, exactly
       as the Licensor published them, NOT on your work. So it does not
       matter how big your work is, how you format it, or how you split,
       join, pad, minify, or rewrite lines in it. For a part of a Covered
       Work that is substantially similar to, but not a line-for-line copy
       of, the Original Work, count all non-blank lines and non-whitespace
       characters of the Original Work that the part corresponds to.

   (c) "Counted files" are the Licensor's own files in the one version you
       took material from. If you took material from more than one
       version, use the smallest of those versions (by the measure being
       applied). Versions are never
       added together. These are never counted, in the Used Portion or in
       the whole: public-domain and third-party material (Section 1(d)),
       lock files, build output, and duplicate copies of a file.

   (d) The Used Portions of everything you and your Affiliates make
       Commercial Use of are added together (each part still counts
       once). You may not get under 5% by splitting material across
       several products, versions, releases, updates, services,
       companies, or people.

   (e) A copy of the whole Original Work, or of most of it, with or
       without changes, always meets the Five Percent Threshold.

   (f) If you make Commercial Use of a Covered Work that includes any
       part of the Original Work, you must, within 30 days of a written
       request from the Licensor that states a reasonable basis for
       concern, let a neutral reviewer chosen by both sides (or by a
       court) compare your work with the Original Work under a
       confidentiality agreement. The reviewer will report only the
       measured percentages. The Licensor pays the reviewer unless the
       threshold is found to be met. If you refuse, a court may infer that
       the threshold is met.

   (g) This measure only decides when commercial permission is needed.
       It does not claim ownership of anything listed in Section 8(b).

